            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Concept of Atomic Nucleus</strong><br>\nRutherford's most famous experiment in 1911 involved the shooting of alpha particles at a thin sheet of gold foil. He discovered that while most particles passed through the foil, some were deflected, leading him to propose a model of the atom with a central nucleus. This overturned the then-prevailing plum pudding model and was a pivotal moment in the field of nuclear physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rutherford's Atomic Model</strong><br>\nBuilding on his discovery of the nucleus, Rutherford hypothesized that the atom consists mostly of empty space, with electrons orbiting around a central, positively charged nucleus. This model explained why atoms were stable and yet could emit radiation, changing into other elements.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rutherford as a Mentor</strong><br>\nRutherford was known for his leadership in the scientific community, mentoring and inspiring a generation of physicists who would go on to make significant scientific advancements of their own. Notable among them was Niels Bohr, who expanded on Rutherford's atomic model.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
