          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Transmutation of Elements</strong><br>\nRutherford's work on the transmutation of elements demonstrated that by bombarding nitrogen with alpha particles, it was possible to create oxygen. This was the first time an element had been transformed into another by artificially induced nuclear reaction, proving that alchemists' old dreams of transmutation had a basis in reality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Honors</strong><br>\nRutherford was appointed the director of the Cavendish Laboratory at Cambridge in 1919, where he oversaw significant breakthroughs in nuclear physics. He was knighted in 1914 and later, in 1931, he was granted a peerage, becoming Baron Rutherford of Nelson. He continued to work and lecture until his death in 1937.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Impact</strong><br>\nErnest Rutherford passed away on October 19, 1937, leaving behind a legacy as the father of nuclear physics. His pioneering work not only transformed our understanding of the atom but also paved the way for future developments in nuclear energy and medicine. His discoveries remain fundamental to the scientific field and continue to influence studies in physics and chemistry today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
